WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.860
So this is a very, very special sample of
lutetium. Again from the bottom

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.029
end of the periodic table, the lanthanides,
and you can see it’s a really

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.420
quite beautiful metallic foil here.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:16.910
So lutetium is the last of the 4f lanthanide
series and it’s also used for

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:17.910
dating meteorites.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:22.181
And, it’s a very small sample. It weighs
a few milligrams but it’s a very,

00:00:22.181 --> 00:00:25.731
very nice sample, stirred under an inert atmosphere
so that we can

00:00:25.731 --> 00:00:29.410
control the chemistry, so it’s full of un-reactive
argon so we can’t form

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.410
the oxide.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.710
Lutetium, although it might not be obvious
to you, is named after a

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.190
European capital city.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:42.350
Lutetium is also used in radionucleotide treatment
for cancers so it’s got

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.120
a very useful role.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:50.070
And, in fact, it’s named after Paris as
Lutetia was the Roman name for

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:58.539
Paris, when it was a Roman city, and therefore
lutetium is one of the few

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:03.720
elements that is named after a city. Some
of the other, rarer, are

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.580
named after cities in Sweden, but lutetium
is the one that is most easily

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:16.110
recognisable, at least by people like me,
who had to learn Latin when we

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.130
were children.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.390
Like thulium it is very expensive so we save
it for special occasions.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.490
When we know that something will absolutely
work for sure then we will

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:26.790
try it with lutetium.

